Let $x$ be the cost of one pencil and $y$ be the cost of one pen. Then we have the following system of equations:

\begin{align*}
5x + y &= 2.50 \\
x + 2y &= 1.85
\end{align*}

We can solve this system of equations by multiplying the second equation by $5$ and subtracting the first equation from it:

\begin{align*}
5(x + 2y) - (5x + y) &= 5(1.85) - 2.50 \\
5x + 10y - 5x - y &= 9.25 - 2.50 \\
9y &= 6.75 \\
y &= \frac{6.75}{9} = 0.75
\end{align*}

Substituting $y = 0.75$ into the first equation, we get:

\begin{align*}
5x + 0.75 &= 2.50 \\
5x &= 2.50 - 0.75 \\
5x &= 1.75 \\
x &= \frac{1.75}{5} = 0.35
\end{align*}

Finally, we can find the cost of two pencils and one pen by substituting $x = 0.35$ and $y = 0.75$ into the expression $2x + y$:

\begin{align*}
2x + y &= 2(0.35) + 0.75 \\
&= 0.70 + 0.75 \\
&= \boxed{1.45}
\end{align*}The answer is: 1.45

Let $p$ be the cost of a pencil and $q$ be the cost of a pen. We are given the following equations:
\begin{align*}
5p + q &= 2.50 \quad \text{(1)} \\
p + 2q &= 1.85 \quad \text{(2)}
\end{align*}
To solve these equations, we can use the method of substitution or elimination. We will use the elimination method. First, we will multiply equation (2) by 5 to make the coefficients of $p$ in both equations the same:
\begin{align*}
5p + 10q &= 9.25 \quad \text{(3)}
\end{align*}
Now, we will subtract equation (1) from equation (3) to eliminate $p$:
\begin{align*}
(5p + 10q) - (5p + q) &= 9.25 - 2.50 \\
9q &= 6.75 \\
q &= \frac{6.75}{9} \\
q &= 0.75
\end{align*}
Now that we know the cost of a pen is $0.75, we can substitute this value into equation (2) to solve for $p$:
\begin{align*}
p + 2(0.75) &= 1.85 \\
p + 1.50 &= 1.85 \\
p &= 1.85 - 1.50 \\
p &= 0.35
\end{align*}
Finally, we can calculate the cost of two pencils and one pen by substituting the values of $p$ and $q$ into the expression $2p + q$:
\begin{align*}
2p + q &= 2(0.35) + 0.75 \\
&= 0.70 + 0.75 \\
&= \boxed{1.45}
\end{align*}
The cost of two pencils and one pen is $\$1.45$.
